// A realization claim about a linked external. `double` has no body here — it
// arrives from `spec_linked_extern_mathlib.inf`, spliced in by the static-merge
// linker under the name section symbol `mathlib::double`.
//
// A bare statement call in a specification body emits `HA_app_ok`, the atom
// that claims the application is *realized*: a total-correctness judgment whose
// conclusion requires the callee's body to reduce to a value stack, so a body
// that trapped would falsify it. `spec_bounds_realization.inf` produces that
// atom over bodies this compiler emitted; here the body whose totality is
// claimed is one the linker produced, at its post-merge ordinal. Nothing else
// in the corpus states a realization claim about foreign code, which is the
// half of the linker envelope a `T_app` alone does not reach — an application
// says what the body computes, `HA_app_ok` says that it computes at all.
//
// The two atoms are emitted together and are both meant: the bare call carries
// the realization claim, and the `assert` keeps the obligation non-vacuous, so
// nothing collapses to the vacuous `HA_true` that P010 rejects.
//
// The claim is true because `double` is total — it is straight-line
// arithmetic with no trap site — which is exactly the property the merged body
// has to keep for this obligation to stay dischargeable.
//
// No data segment: the merged module built from this one is compiled by `coqc`,
// and the corpus rejects a data segment in any module it gates.

external fn double(x: i32) -> i32;
use { double } from mathlib;

pub fn twice(x: i32) -> i32 {
  return double(x);
}

spec RealizedExtern {
  fn calling_is_realized() forall {
    let x: i32 = @;
    double(x);
    assert(double(x) == x + x);
  }
}
